    "text": "96\n\n Make This Your Last Miserable Tax Season Ever\nSara,\nIt\u2019s the day before the deadline and in the last few weeks I\u2019ve been getting A LOT of emails and calls from former clients saying \u2018I\u2019m never doing this again, find me a buyer or I\u2019m burning it down\u2019\nIf the thought of one more tax season makes you want to lock the door and throw your laptop in the ocean\u2026 you\u2019re not alone.\nHere\u2019s the good news: You\u2019ve got options. Whether you want to offload your firm completely or just lighten your load, now\u2019s the time to act\u2014not mid-February when you\u2019re buried in 1099s and caffeine. I know you\u2019re tired and you simply cannot add on, but save this email for next week because May is the perfect time to make sure you do not do this to yourself yet again.\u00a0\nHere\u2019s your \u201cget-out-of-misery\u201d menu:\n1. Sell Your Firm and Walk Away\nIf you\u2019re done-done\u2014burned out, over it, and ready for the next chapter\u2014you can sell 100% of your firm and exit entirely. With the right buyer and a clean transition plan, you\u2019ll likely get paid for what you\u2019ve built and never have to file another extension (except your own from your sailboat)\n2. Sell Your Firm and Stay On (Without the Stress)\nHand off ownership, keep your favorite clients, and show up like a normal employee\u2014minus the late nights, payroll stress, or management headaches. Many buyers offer rollover equity, letting you cash out partially now and still benefit from future growth or resale.\n3. Sell a Majority, Keep a Minority Stake\nThis is the \"have your cake and eat it too\" option. Sell a majority stake to a partner who takes over operations, while you keep a seat at the table (and some future upside). Can be perfect if you still love the work but not the grind of running the firm.\n4. Merge with a Larger Firm (and Sell into It)\nJoin forces with a larger, better-resourced firm. You sell your book into theirs, get support with staffing and tech, and possibly take a leadership or client service role post-merger. This often comes with equity or bonus payouts tied to performance.\n5. Sell Internally with SBA-Backed Financing\nLet a trusted employee or junior partner buy you out over time using SBA financing. You\u2019ll phase out at your pace while mentoring the next generation. Bonus: you can structure this to get your full value and a smoother transition for clients.\n6. Package Your Firm for a Search Fund Buyer\nThere\u2019s a growing pool of motivated buyers\u2014often MBAs with investor backing\u2014who are looking to buy and run firms just like yours. You sell, help them get up to speed for a few months, and then hand over the reins while keeping some seller financing or equity in the deal.\nIf even one of those options sounds like something you need to do, don\u2019t wait until you\u2019re staring down the barrel of another tax season... reply to this email and let\u2019s make a plan.\nTo a smooth tax season,\nSara Sharp\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nBusiness Consultant & Founder of Deal Academy\nP.S. If you're looking to buy or sell an accounting firm, and want me to keep an eye out for you, fill out this form here.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2025\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
